================================================================================
CONSOLE ADD-ONS RESEARCH
(For dedicated add-ons section — consoles that extended lifespan via hardware)
================================================================================

CONCEPT:
Add-ons were hardware peripherals that plugged into a base console to extend its
capabilities — usually adding a CD-ROM drive, extra processing power, or new
storage media. These were sold separately and often positioned as a cheaper
upgrade path vs. buying an entirely new console.

Notable add-ons by parent console:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SEGA MEGA DRIVE / GENESIS:
  • Mega CD / Sega CD (1991 JP / 1992 NA)
    - Adds CD-ROM drive underneath/alongside the Genesis
    - Added FMV playback, CD audio, faster CPU (second 68000), extra RAM
    - JP model 1 sat below Genesis; NA/model 2 sat to the side
    - ~6 million units sold combined
    - Compatible games: Sonic CD, Final Fight CD, Night Trap

  • 32X (1994, worldwide)
    - Slots into Genesis cartridge port
    - Added two SH2 32-bit processors, boosted to 32-bit graphics
    - Required own power supply + AV cable pass-through
    - ~800,000 units sold — widely considered a commercial failure
    - Only 40 games released

  • Mega CD + 32X combined = "Mega CD 32X" (very few games made for this combo)

FAMICOM (Japan only):
  • Famicom Disk System / FDS (1986 JP)
    - Floppy disk drive add-on sat below Famicom via RAM adapter
    - Larger game storage than cartridges, rewritable disks
    - Added extra sound channels (Zelda, Castlevania, Metroid all used FDS audio)
    - Never released outside Japan
    - Discontinued 2003 (disk writing kiosks)

GAME BOY (on SNES):
  • Super Game Boy (1994, worldwide)
    - Cartridge adapter that plugged into SNES cartridge slot
    - Played original Game Boy (and later Game Boy Color) games on TV
    - Added color palettes, border frames, special Super Game Boy-enhanced modes
    - Super Game Boy 2 (1998, Japan only) added link cable port

SNES:
  • Satellaview (1995, Japan only)
    - Satellite modem add-on for Super Famicom (SNES)
    - Downloaded games and broadcasts via BS satellite service
    - Time-limited exclusive content (some now lost forever)
    - Rare and historically significant

NINTENDO 64:
  • 64DD (1999, Japan only)
    - Magnetic disk drive add-on for N64
    - Larger storage, rewritable disks, real-time clock
    - 10 games released, service ended 2001 — massive commercial failure
    - Planned NA/EU release cancelled

  • Expansion Pak (1998, worldwide)
    - RAM upgrade from 4 MB to 8 MB
    - Required for some games (Majora's Mask, Donkey Kong 64, Perfect Dark)
    - Improved resolution/draw distance in compatible games
    - Landmark: first RAM upgrade for a home console sold at retail

GAMECUBE:
  • Game Boy Player (2003, worldwide)
    - Attached to bottom of GameCube
    - Played Game Boy, Game Boy Color, and Game Boy Advance cartridges on TV
    - Required startup disc
    - Popular with collectors today

XBOX 360 / XBOX ONE:
  • Kinect (Xbox 360 version, 2010 worldwide)
    - Depth-sensing camera + microphone array
    - Markerless full-body motion and voice control
    - 35 million units sold — fastest-selling consumer electronics device on record at
      that time
    - Kinect 2.0 (Xbox One, 2013): improved accuracy, mandatory in original bundle,
      later made optional

PLAYSTATION 2:
  • EyeToy (2003 EU/NA)
    - USB camera accessory
    - Motion detection via color tracking, not depth sensing
    - 10.5 million units sold
    - Precursor to PlayStation Eye and PlayStation Move

PLAYSTATION 3 / 4:
  • PlayStation Move (2010, PS3 / 2016 bundle with PS4)
    - Motion controller (wand + navigation controller), uses PS Eye/Camera
    - Required for PlayStation VR

  • PlayStation VR / PSVR (2016, PS4)
    - First-party VR headset for PS4
    - 5 million units sold
    - Requires PS Camera + PS Move or DualShock 4
    - 1920×1080 OLED display (960×1080 per eye), 120Hz

  • PlayStation VR2 / PSVR2 (2023, PS5)
    - Single-cable USB-C connection to PS5
    - 2000×2040 per eye OLED, eye tracking, adaptive trigger controllers
    - No backward compatibility with PSVR1 games

NES / FAMICOM:
  • R.O.B. / Family Computer Robot (1985 NA / 1984 JP)
    - Robotic Operating Buddy — small robot that interacted with on-screen cues
    - Only 2 games supported it: Gyromite and Stack-Up
    - Used as a marketing device to get NES into toy stores rather than electronics
      stores after the 1983 crash
    - Historically significant: without ROB, NES may never have launched in NA

ATARI 2600:
  • Atari 2600 Voice Module / AtariVox (3rd party, 2004 — yes, 2004)
    - Speech synthesizer + EEPROM storage created by the homebrew community
    - Adds voice synthesis and save game capability to Atari 2600
    - Not official Atari hardware — included for collector interest

PROPOSED DATA MODEL FIELD:
  addons: [
    {
      name: string,
      year: number,
      region: string,          // "JP" | "NA" | "EU" | "Worldwide"
      type: string,            // "Media Drive" | "Processor" | "Motion" | "VR" | "Memory" | "Accessory"
      units_sold: string,      // e.g. "800,000" or "~6 million"
      notable_games: string[], // games that required or benefited from this add-on
      description: string,
      lifespan_extended: boolean  // did it meaningfully extend console lifespan?
    }
  ]

